## Proof

We evaluate $\displaystyle L = \lim_{n\to\infty} \exp(-n^2) \sum_{j=n}^{4n} \frac{n^j}{j!}$.

**Upper bound.** Since every term $\frac{n^j}{j!}$ is non-negative, we have

$$0 \;\le\; \sum_{j=n}^{4n} \frac{n^j}{j!} \;\le\; \sum_{j=0}^{\infty} \frac{n^j}{j!} \;=\; e^n.$$

Multiplying by $\exp(-n^2) > 0$:

$$0 \;\le\; \exp(-n^2)\sum_{j=n}^{4n} \frac{n^j}{j!} \;\le\; \exp(-n^2)\cdot e^n \;=\; e^{\,n - n^2} \;=\; e^{-n(n-1)}.$$

**Limit of the bound.** As $n \to \infty$, $n(n-1) \to \infty$, so

$$e^{-n(n-1)} \longrightarrow 0.$$

**Conclusion.** The expression is squeezed between $0$ and a quantity tending to $0$. By the squeeze theorem:

$$\lim_{n\to\infty} \exp(-n^2) \sum_{j=n}^{4n} \frac{n^j}{j!} = 0.$$

